e race party meanwhile was going  full tilt  the menu was designed to fit in with the  location  it's all very brightly colored it's all  about having fun and that goes for  everything from the setup of the tent  here to each and every dish we served  one item on the menu was variations on  golden beets  as long as he doesn't serve red beets  the sheer numbers of celebrity guests  made this year's kids race party a  record setter 1200 people reveled well  into the wee hours of the morning  records are enjoying a comeback and not  just for their retro sound the latest  trend that has hipsters in a spin is  eyewear made out of vinyl a creation by  zach tipton and it all started off in  his dad's garage it was there that his  father stored his old record collection  and where zach came up with the idea to  recycle records as a new and fashionable  product we visited the pioneer of specs  appeal in budapest  zach tipton puts a new spin on records  nobody listens to anymore  but his vinyl isn't for the ears  but for the eyes  vinyl eyeglass frames  they're in the groove literally  the models carry famous names from music  jackson lennon and miles their visual  aids and sun protection  they're the recycled frames of the vinyl  eyes brand and they're more than just a  fashion statement  every time somebody somebody puts on the  frame they know what they're wearing and  i hope it makes them  behave in a more  environmentally responsible fashion you  know and when they think that something  that they're wearing  is inspired by that then it'll influence  their behavior  zak tipton studio in budapest is stacked  full of old phonograph records  when laminated and cut out with a jigsaw  they become eyeglass frames  every frame is made here by hand just as  the first model was in the year 2000  i wanted to make some some frames out of  a different material than metal and i  was in the workshop and i kind of looked  over in the corner and i saw my father's  record collection  and i was like ah why don't i just try  making them out of that and i took a  couple and glued them up and cut them  out and then that's how the idea was  born  zak tipton grew up in the united states  but since 2004 he's been designing and  producing in hungary his mother's home  country  now he's got 15 employees to help him  he finds his raw material in various  record stores around the hungarian  capital  zach tipton is also a record collector  in private  for his eyewear he generally takes the  leftover stock on the bottom shelf  if it's not coming from here it'll come  from old um  distributors that went out of business  they couldn't sell the records the  market collapsed factories went out of  business and they can't send them back  for recycling so these are tens of  thousands of records just sitting in  warehouses  a few hardcore vinyl fans tend to view  tipton's designs with skepticism  i'm not too keen on it i like records  and i think records should be listened  to or maybe hang them up on a wall  i find it a bit morbid to make things  out of them  other music fans are enchanted with the  idea among them elton john hollywood  star michael madsen rapper flow reader  the band 30 seconds to mars and american  movie director quentin tarantino  music stars are both customers and  inspiration for the frames  what we like to do is just take a  musician and examine the the history  that he he lived in  uh what kind of style he wore or if we  were to make jimi hendrix some uh a  frame  how would that frame look would jimi  hendrix wear this or would jim morrison  wear this frame  irish singer-songwriter bono has ordered  a set of frames zach tipton picked out a  very special record as his raw material  it's a vinyl that was pressed in in the  70s  and he went to this concert that uh  collected funds for amnesty  international and he attended this in  june of 1979  and the research i did showed that he  was very influenced by this  zak tipton's company sells as many as  500 frames a month to customers from  around the world the frames start at 300  euros the cases also made a vinyl are  included and a spectacle stand  sometimes people get really upset  because of the cultural damage hey  you're destroying great music and what  about these artists and all that  but you'll people have to realize that  that just like books or anything that  society creates not every one of them is  going to be a masterpiece culturally not  every book is going to become a  classical piece  same with vinyl  zach tipton has found a lucrative new  use for old and unwanted vinyl and given  a whole new meaning to the word groovy  that was some of our favorite highlights  of the week and that is it from me and  o'donnell and the rest of the euromax  crew for today catch up with us online  for more information thanks for watching  bye  you
 

